WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.230
- Welcome back everybody.

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:02.700
I'm hanging out with Jenna Lyons.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.924
Give it up!
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:00:05.924 --> 00:00:08.520
So next year, athletes
from around the world

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.640
will gather in Paris
for the 2024 Olympics.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.250
My next guest is a retired
Olympian and world champion

00:00:14.250 --> 00:00:15.870
and fencing.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.630
She's training the next
generation of Olympic hopefuls,

00:00:18.630 --> 00:00:20.460
but that's only part of her story.

00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.680
More impressive, she's
providing mentorship

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.240
and leadership to underserved kids

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:25.680
right here in New York City.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.039
Please welcome Nzingha Prescod everybody.

00:00:28.039 --> 00:00:29.205
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:00:29.205 --> 00:00:30.610
Meet Jenna.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:31.677
- Hi!
- Hi!

00:00:31.677 --> 00:00:32.830
- I'm so glad you're here.
- Yeah, thank you.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:36.270
- So, we didn't plan this,
but you fenced as well.

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:37.301
You fenced in high school, right?

00:00:37.301 --> 00:00:39.270
- I fenced in, I mean I
fenced in high school.

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:40.103
Let's, between.

00:00:40.103 --> 00:00:40.936
- I know, but I didn't even know

00:00:40.936 --> 00:00:42.540
what fencing really was in high school.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:43.860
Like, it wasn't like a thing,

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:45.690
like an opportunity for me as a kid.

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:46.650
I never even knew about it.

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:48.180
So I find that incredible
that you both did that.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.010
- I went to for like
a heartbeat, a little,

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:51.780
a private French school
and then I transferred out.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:54.593
But they were very into
fencing and I liked the outfit.

00:00:54.593 --> 00:00:56.253
- Yeah, it's pretty chique.

00:00:56.253 --> 00:00:58.560
- [Jenna] It's very chique.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:00.000
- But you grew up fencing, right?

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.320
- I did grow up fencing.

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:02.460
So I grew up in Brooklyn.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:04.620
I grew up in an area of
Brooklyn called Flatlands,

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.930
which is right outside of Flatbush,

00:01:06.930 --> 00:01:08.100
predominantly Caribbean.

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:10.020
My mom raised my sister and I,

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:12.840
and she always put us in a lot of sports.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:14.580
She's very socially conscious

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.700
and really believes in black excellence.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:20.640
And so she learned about Peter Westbrook,

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:22.230
who is a six time Olympian

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.050
and bronze medalist at 1984.

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:28.380
And so he was sending a
lot of inner city youth

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:31.507
to the Olympics and so
she was like, "Perfect!

00:01:31.507 --> 00:01:32.760
"I'm gonna send my girls there."

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:35.250
And so we commuted from Flatlands,

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:36.780
which is in Outer Brooklyn,

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:39.510
all the way up to Chelsea, Manhattan.

00:01:39.510 --> 00:01:42.450
And as I was getting good, it
was four or five times a week.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.190
So it was quite a commitment.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:46.230
It was pretty successful.
- You have super mom.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:47.940
- I have a super mom.
- Yeah.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:48.773
- Bless my mommy.

00:01:48.773 --> 00:01:49.606
She is so great.
- Yeah.

00:01:49.606 --> 00:01:50.580
And you said she's a single mama?

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:51.660
- She's a single mom.
- Oh my gosh,

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:52.560
holding all that down

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:54.120
and taking you to all
those things, that's a lot.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.290
- Right, and from there

00:01:55.290 --> 00:01:57.000
I was able to work with the Olympic coach

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.200
since I was 10.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.900
And that allowed me to win
world championship medals,

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:02.220
go to two Olympics,

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.580
and have a really
phenomenal fencing career.

00:02:05.580 --> 00:02:06.990
Next year I'll be in the Hall of Fame,

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:08.257
so that's pretty awesome.
- Oh my gosh!

00:02:08.257 --> 00:02:10.407
Congratulations!
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:02:10.407 --> 00:02:13.250
That's incredible.
- Yeah.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:14.310
An incredible journey.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:17.010
Fencing has really changed my life a ton

00:02:17.010 --> 00:02:18.810
and enabled a lot of opportunities.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:19.643
So it's--

00:02:19.643 --> 00:02:21.540
- And it builds, I think for
kids, when you find something,

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:22.373
I always say this on the show,

00:02:22.373 --> 00:02:23.850
but when you find something you're good at

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.730
and you feel good about and special,

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:28.380
it gives you a different
level of confidence.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.327
- Confidence, right?
- Yeah.

00:02:29.327 --> 00:02:31.650
- And that you can do, can do, can do.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.630
Yeah, and you take that
with you everywhere, so.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:34.463
- Yeah.
- That's been--

00:02:34.463 --> 00:02:36.690
- It totally transfers over
in other parts of your life.

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:37.650
I agree.

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:39.870
So explain fencing 'cause I had to do it,

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:41.977
like I said for "The Voice"
one time, and I was like,

00:02:41.977 --> 00:02:43.027
"I'm sorry, what?

00:02:43.027 --> 00:02:44.250
"You want me to what?"

00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:46.830
You know, explain that if nobody
had ever like heard of it,

00:02:46.830 --> 00:02:48.360
which a lot of people haven't, like--

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.430
- It's incredibly technical.

00:02:50.430 --> 00:02:53.010
It's, people call it athletic chess.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.191
- It is, yeah.
- Right, it's very physical.

00:02:55.191 --> 00:02:57.930
But even more so than
physical, it's very mental.

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:58.763
- It's like strategy.

00:02:58.763 --> 00:03:00.990
- Right, it's totally critical thinking,

00:03:00.990 --> 00:03:03.180
analyzing, problem solving.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:04.590
So there's like this maze

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:07.740
that you're constantly trying
to figure out the intricacies

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.750
of how to change this
detail or that detail

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.790
so that you can battle your opponent

00:03:11.790 --> 00:03:13.350
and come out victorious.

00:03:13.350 --> 00:03:15.750
So it's a really amazing,
well-rounded sport.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:16.590
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:03:16.590 --> 00:03:19.888
- It normally attracts a
lot of affluent people.

00:03:19.888 --> 00:03:22.140
Prep schools, Ivy League schools.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:25.080
- That's why I never heard
of it when I was young, yeah.

00:03:25.080 --> 00:03:26.790
- So, like, it allowed
me to go to Columbia,

00:03:26.790 --> 00:03:28.200
fence on Columbia's team.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.300
And just opens a lot of doors.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.240
And so you can imagine
it's quite expensive.

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.490
And so that makes it pretty exclusionary

00:03:35.490 --> 00:03:38.040
in terms of geography, finances,

00:03:38.040 --> 00:03:40.140
even culturally, it's pretty hard

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:43.470
to immerse a community in fencing

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:46.560
when historically it's been
a predominantly white sport.

00:03:46.560 --> 00:03:48.580
So that's the background.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.677
- Yeah, that's like a rich
people sport where I'm like,

00:03:50.677 --> 00:03:52.500
they were like, "Yeah, I played polo."

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:56.130
I'm like, "Okay, I played
volleyball," I was like,

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:59.160
I don't like all the public
school things you do.

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:00.630
Well explain your organization.

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:01.860
Like why is this important to you?

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:04.050
- Right, so I grew up
with this opportunity,

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:06.120
but I was an anomaly in accessing that.

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:08.550
And so I started the Prescott Institute

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:10.230
for Sport Teamwork and Education.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.150
We started as fencing in the park,

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:13.230
which is a summer program.

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:16.410
And so we just, I wanted to
increase access to the sport

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:18.720
in my community where I grew up

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:22.020
because I did have that huge
kind of burden of traveling.

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:23.730
And so I always wanted to bring it back

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:26.820
with all this skill I
acquired over 20 plus years

00:04:26.820 --> 00:04:29.880
and open up those doors for
the youth in my community.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:30.990
- The kid that was you, yeah.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.280
- Yes, for the kids that were me,

00:04:32.280 --> 00:04:35.220
because I was the only one
growing up who had that.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:39.660
And even just quality, organized
sports are very expensive.

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.460
And where I grew up, it's not accessible.

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:44.190
And so that was just always
something I wanted to bring back

00:04:44.190 --> 00:04:45.780
and put in my neighborhood

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:48.870
so that breaking down all those barriers

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.010
for the next generation.

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:51.030
- And it gives you confidence,

00:04:51.030 --> 00:04:54.180
but it also, it keeps kids out of trouble.

00:04:54.180 --> 00:04:55.290
That's why my mom, you know,

00:04:55.290 --> 00:04:56.730
I was always in sports,
I was always in theater.

00:04:56.730 --> 00:04:57.840
I was always involved in something

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.111
and that's why I stayed outta trouble.

00:05:00.111 --> 00:05:01.770
- Idle hands, yeah.
- Idle hands, right.

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:03.540
And it's productive outlet for them.

00:05:03.540 --> 00:05:04.410
It's productive.

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:05.940
You learn strong work ethic.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.540
I always tell people I had
to learn how to work hard.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:10.860
So that's what we teach them.

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.480
We teach them to have strong characters,

00:05:12.480 --> 00:05:15.540
to be good leaders, to be good people.

00:05:15.540 --> 00:05:19.320
So we heavily instill
Olympic values of excellence,

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.020
friendship, and respect in the program.

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:26.010
And just hope they'll use
it in their next steps

00:05:26.010 --> 00:05:26.843
and whatever--
- They will

00:05:26.843 --> 00:05:27.990
and they won't even know they're doing it.

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:29.760
There are things that I was
so annoyed by when I was a kid

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:31.500
and I was like, "Ugh! Why
do I have to do this?"

00:05:31.500 --> 00:05:33.877
And then now I'd see it in my work ethic.

00:05:33.877 --> 00:05:35.505
You know, you didn't really
see it when you were a kid.

00:05:35.505 --> 00:05:36.690
- It pays it forward.

00:05:36.690 --> 00:05:37.595
- Yeah, agreed.
- Right.

00:05:37.595 --> 00:05:38.700
- All right.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.830
Joining us now are Julia and Amy,

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:42.690
two of Nzingha fencing students

00:05:42.690 --> 00:05:44.580
from her non-profit school in Brooklyn.

00:05:44.580 --> 00:05:46.457
Welcome to the show, ladies.

00:05:46.457 --> 00:05:47.765
- Hi!
- Hi!

00:05:47.765 --> 00:05:49.987
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:05:49.987 --> 00:05:51.420
- So I have to ask,

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.760
what's your favorite part of fencing?

00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:58.760
- My favorite part of fencing
is that I make lots of friends

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:02.520
and we go through things
and once I beat my brother,

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:05.310
he was a little bit mad.
(audience laughs)

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:07.860
- Oh my God, you're just like my kids.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:08.693
That's amazing.

00:06:08.693 --> 00:06:09.690
I love the competition.

00:06:09.690 --> 00:06:10.560
What about you?

00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:12.150
- My favorite part of fencing

00:06:12.150 --> 00:06:14.760
is the fact that I get to work
with kids that can relate to,

00:06:14.760 --> 00:06:17.580
and it helps me balance being in a school

00:06:17.580 --> 00:06:18.840
and being an athlete.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:20.910
- Yeah, I love that too.
(audience applauds)

00:06:20.910 --> 00:06:22.160
Like those relationships.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:24.990
So I have to ask you this,

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:25.823
I wanted to ask a minute ago,

00:06:25.823 --> 00:06:27.330
but how do you afford the non-profit?

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:28.320
Like how do you do this?

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:29.430
Like, it's a lot of money.

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.800
- Right, so the program
is free for families.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:35.160
So equipment, lessons, classes,

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.740
all of our services for families are free.

00:06:37.740 --> 00:06:40.200
So we depend on funders.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:41.340
Individual funders,

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:43.980
we get some funding from the City Council.

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:45.574
So very thankful.

00:06:45.574 --> 00:06:47.280
Some corporate sponsors.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:49.830
So I'm an employee at Ernst and Young, EY,

00:06:49.830 --> 00:06:52.680
so they've been very
supportive of the program,

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.990
allowing me to work
full-time on building it.

00:06:54.990 --> 00:06:58.350
And then Ballet's Corporation
has been super helpful.

00:06:58.350 --> 00:06:59.730
And then non-profits,

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:02.550
like Women's Sport Foundation
has been really incredible.

00:07:02.550 --> 00:07:04.710
We're actually going
to be there later today

00:07:04.710 --> 00:07:05.880
to accept an award.

00:07:05.880 --> 00:07:08.700
So it's really community effort where

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:11.070
it's been very active and
the team's very active

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:14.340
to find funding and so we're
not yet going national,

00:07:14.340 --> 00:07:16.470
but hopefully this year
we'll go to the nationals.

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:17.820
And then from there
it's international, so.

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:19.890
- It's the only thing
holding you back really

00:07:19.890 --> 00:07:21.060
the financial part of it
when you say nationals--

00:07:21.060 --> 00:07:24.180
- Right, financials are
like, we all work very hard.

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:25.320
- I was the kid that went on scholarship

00:07:25.320 --> 00:07:27.510
to anything in high school like
'cause we didn't have money.

00:07:27.510 --> 00:07:28.343
So this matters.
- It really matters.

00:07:28.343 --> 00:07:31.110
- And it really does change
a person's trajectory

00:07:31.110 --> 00:07:32.460
like their life.
- Right, exactly.

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:34.200
So we're trying to give them the best

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:35.610
and they deserve the best, so.

00:07:35.610 --> 00:07:37.830
- Absolutely.
- We depend on the community.

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:39.030
- All right, well I was thinking,

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:41.460
Amy, you want to teach us
like a move or something?

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.537
I mean, the swords here,
I'm here, you're here.

00:07:43.537 --> 00:07:45.640
- Of course.
- Okay, let's do it.

00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:47.093
Okay, let's stand up.
(audience cheers and applauds)

00:07:47.093 --> 00:07:48.993
You want to do it?
- Yes.

00:07:48.993 --> 00:07:50.640
(audience applauds)

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:52.080
- I heard there's a specific way

00:07:52.080 --> 00:07:53.280
we're supposed to get a sword.

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:54.113
Is that right?

00:07:54.113 --> 00:07:55.170
- Well, first things first,

00:07:55.170 --> 00:07:57.649
you can never have the
tip be in the air, right.

00:07:57.649 --> 00:07:58.482
- Okay.

00:07:58.482 --> 00:07:59.790
- 'Cause that's a safety hazard.

00:07:59.790 --> 00:08:01.380
So you wanna keep your tip down.

00:08:01.380 --> 00:08:03.570
And I think Amy knows how
to hold the foil pretty well

00:08:03.570 --> 00:08:04.645
so she can show you.

00:08:04.645 --> 00:08:05.478
- Okay.

00:08:05.478 --> 00:08:06.466
- Do you want to show us, en garde?

00:08:06.466 --> 00:08:10.410
- Okay, so we're gonna
do the advanced lunge.

00:08:10.410 --> 00:08:14.370
So I'm going to put my foot forward

00:08:14.370 --> 00:08:15.870
and then bring my other foot.

00:08:15.870 --> 00:08:18.810
Then we put the pressure on
my other foot and we lunge.

00:08:18.810 --> 00:08:20.340
- Ooh, looks like a workout, Amy.

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:21.173
Okay.

00:08:21.173 --> 00:08:22.380
Okay, okay, okay.

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:23.213
- In heels.

00:08:23.213 --> 00:08:24.046
That's a plus.

00:08:24.046 --> 00:08:24.960
- That's all right.

00:08:24.960 --> 00:08:25.793
That's all right, we're women.

00:08:25.793 --> 00:08:26.760
We do it all the time.

00:08:26.760 --> 00:08:28.937
So what about, when do I get my sword?

00:08:28.937 --> 00:08:30.754
(everyone laughs)

00:08:30.754 --> 00:08:31.860
When do I get that?

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:32.693
Are we gonna play with that?

00:08:32.693 --> 00:08:33.900
We're not gonna play with that?

00:08:33.900 --> 00:08:35.340
Okay, I want one, I want one.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:36.840
I was like, "Can I have one?"

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:38.528
Do you have to, you grab
it a certain way, right?

00:08:38.528 --> 00:08:39.491
- Yes, I'll grab one, yes.

00:08:39.491 --> 00:08:40.324
- Okay.

00:08:40.324 --> 00:08:41.248
- So grab it by.
- So it's facing down.

00:08:41.248 --> 00:08:42.300
- This is called a pommel.
- Well, I'm the host.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:43.140
I have to hand them out.

00:08:43.140 --> 00:08:45.067
Yeah, I was like, "Here you go.

00:08:45.067 --> 00:08:46.287
"There you go."
- Oh, thank you, miss.

00:08:46.287 --> 00:08:48.600
- I'm a good host.
(audience laughs)

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:49.897
- I'll grab one too.
- Okay.

00:08:49.897 --> 00:08:50.730
- Okay, okay.

00:08:50.730 --> 00:08:53.610
Okay, so you stand like this, right?

00:08:53.610 --> 00:08:56.753
And your foot is is perpendicular?

00:08:56.753 --> 00:08:57.702
Okay.
- Yes.

